d geographic level matter?: the public health disparities geocoding project</article-title><source>Am J Epidemiol</source><year>2002</year><volume>156</volume><fpage>471</fpage><lpage>82</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12196317</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/aje/kwf068</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Krieger</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Waterman</surname><given-names>PD</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>JT</given-names></name><name><surname>Soobader</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Subramanian</surname><given-names>SV</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Monitoring socioeconomic inequalities in sexually transmitted infections, tuberculosis, and violence: geocoding and choice of area-based socioeconomic measures&#x02013;the public health disparities geocoding project (US)</article-title><source>Public Health Rep</source><year>2003</year><volume>118</volume><fpage>240</fpage><lpage>60</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12766219</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Singh</surname><given-names>GK</given-names></name><name><surname>Miller</surname><given-names>BA</given-names></name><name><surname>Hankey</surname><given-names>BF</given-names></name><name><surname>Edwards</surname><given-names>BK</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Persistent area socioeconomic disparities in U.S. incidence of cervical cancer, mortality, stage, and survival, 1975&#x02013;2000</article-title><source>Cancer</source><year>2004</year><volume>101</volume><fpage>1051</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15329915</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/cncr.20467</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Krieger</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Williams</surname><given-names>DR</given-names></name><name><surname>Moss</surname><given-names>NE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Measuring social class in US public health research: concepts, methodologies, and guidelines</article-title><source>Annu Rev Public Health</source><year>1997</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>341</fpage><lpage>78</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9143723</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1146/annurev.publhealth.18.1.341</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kaplan</surname><given-names>GA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Part III summary: what is the role of social environment in understating inequalities in health?</article-title><source>Annals of New York Academy of Sciences</source><year>1999</year><volume>896</volume><fpage>116</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1749-6632.1999.tb08109.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kaplan</surname><given-names>GA</given-names></name><name><surname>Pamuk</surname><given-names>ER</given-names></name><name><surname>Lynch</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name><name><surname>Cohen</surname><given-names>RD</given-names></name><name><surname>Balfour</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Inequality in income and mortality in the United States: analysis of mortality and potential pathways</article-title><source>BMJ</source><year>1996</year><volume>312</volume><fpage>999</fpage><lpage>1003</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8616393</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Barr</surname><given-names>RG</given-names></name><name><surname>Diez Roux</surnam